---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ErrorFormatGenerator from '../../components/ErrorFormatGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="API Error Response Format Generator" description="Build a consistent, structured error response JSON shape for your API.">
  <ErrorFormatGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why error format consistency matters</h2>
  <p>An API where every endpoint returns errors in a slightly different shape forces client code to handle each one specially. A single, predictable error format lets client applications write one generic error handler that works everywhere.</p>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Define your error code, message, and any field-level details.</li>
    <li>Copy the generated JSON structure.</li>
    <li>Apply this same shape consistently across all your API's error responses.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>A validation error might return a machine-readable code like <code>VALIDATION_ERROR</code>, a human-readable message, the relevant HTTP status, and a details array pinpointing exactly which field failed and why.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Returning different error shapes from different endpoints</strong>, defeating the purpose of having a format at all.</li>
    <li><strong>Only including a human-readable message</strong> without a machine-readable code, forcing client code to parse error strings to determine what went wrong.</li>
    <li><strong>Leaking internal details</strong> (stack traces, database errors) in production error responses, which can expose sensitive implementation details.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Establishing a consistent error contract before building out a new API</li>
    <li>Standardizing error handling across an existing API with inconsistent error formats</li>
    <li>Documenting the error shape for API consumers</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is there an official standard for API error formats?</strong> RFC 7807 (Problem Details for HTTP APIs) is one widely referenced standard, though many APIs use their own conventions — consistency within your own API matters most.</p>
</ToolLayout>
